{
 "doc": {
  "what": "Golden numbers: what ryagram/maprace/localarea.py (compute_numbers) returns for nine chosen clicks on a MADE-UP place. A second implementation (GEO's JavaScript) is right when it reproduces them as stated under 'tolerance'.",
  "distance": "nearest_km, nearest[].km, within: the geodesic distance on the WGS84 ellipsoid (a = 6378137 m, 1/f = 298.257223563), computed by pyproj Geod.inv (Karney), NOT a sphere and NOT haversine. nearest_km and nearest[].km are in km rounded to 4 decimals (Python round, half to even on the binary float). within[r] = the number of points whose UNROUNDED distance is <= r km (a point at exactly r counts). nearest = the five points of smallest distance, ties kept in file order (points are sorted by id first). A click exactly on a point gives km 0.0. Points outside every in-county block group are dropped before anything is counted ('points' below are the ones kept).",
  "sphere_note": "A sphere changes distances: with the mean radius R = 6371.0088 km, haversine d = 2R asin(sqrt(sin^2(dlat/2) + cos(lat1) cos(lat2) sin^2(dlon/2))), the golden's nearest_km differs by up to 'sphere_reference_summary.max_abs_diff_nearest_km' on these cases (about 0.3 per cent). Strict equality to 4 decimals needs the ellipsoid; see tolerance.",
  "area": "units[].area_km2 is CALCULATED by the engine from the polygon in the map's equal-area projection (Albers, ryagram.maprace.geometry.PROJECTION): |sum of the signed shoelace areas of all rings| / 1e6. Outer rings run clockwise and holes anticlockwise in the file, so a hole subtracts. A page can reproduce it to about 0.3 per cent with the spherical-excess or any equal-area formula; it is not used for the checks below except through people_est.",
  "people_est": "people_est[r] = ESTIMATED people within r km of the click = sum over in-county block groups that have a population of pop x share, where share = (area of the block group inside the disc of radius r km round the click) / (the block group's area_km2), clipped to 0..1; people are assumed even inside a block group. A block group with no population (null) adds nothing. The engine measures the inside area by counting cells of r/200 km in the click-centred azimuthal-equidistant projection (true distances from the click), after deciding whole-in (farthest vertex <= r) and whole-out exactly. Rounding of the total: if total >= 100 round to the nearest 10 (Python round(x, -1), half to even) else to the nearest whole number. NOTE: a block group is NOT all-in or all-out by its centre; the share is by area.",
  "ring_leaves_place": "ring_leaves_place[r] = true when the disc of radius r reaches past the loaded county: county_edge_km < r, where county_edge_km is the shortest straight (azimuthal-equidistant) distance from the click to an 'exterior edge' = an edge of an in-county block group that no other in-county block group shares (holes included; edges are matched by their two vertices rounded to 1e-7 degree, undirected). The list is in 'exterior_edges'. county_edge_km is rounded to 4 decimals. A click on the line has edge 0. Other-county block groups (in_place = false) are context only: they are not loaded, so a ring reaching them leaves the place.",
  "click_block_group": "The id of the first unit in file order, with in_place true, whose rings hold the click (ray casting, even-odd over all its rings so holes work; a point is inside when (yi > y) != (yj > y) and x < (xj - xi)(y - yi)/(yj - yi) + xi, summed by parity). A click exactly on a shared boundary goes to the first matching unit by that rule.",
  "points": "Points are the fast_food nodes of osm_food_county.json (amenity = fast_food only, a restaurant or a bar is ignored), kept when inside an in-county block group. All names are made up.",
  "nearest_caveat": "nearest_may_miss_across_edge = (nearest_km is null or nearest_km > county_edge_km): a nearer place across the county line is not loaded, so the page must say so."
 },
 "radii_km": [
  1.0,
  2.0,
  5.0
 ],
 "category": "fast_food",
 "place_folder": "tests/fixtures/perfect_city_made_up",
